def last(arr, target, start, end):
    # Base case: if start is greater than end, return the next index
    if start > end:
        return start

    # Calculate the middle index, use integer division
    mid = start + (end - start) // 2

    # If the middle element is the target, move to the right part
    if arr[mid] == target:
        return last(arr, target, mid + 1, end)

    # If the middle element is greater than the target, move to the left part
    if arr[mid] > target:
        return last(arr, target, start, mid - 1)

    # If the middle element is less than the target, move to the right part
    if arr[mid] < target:
        return last(arr, target, mid + 1, end)
```

This function utilizes binary search to efficiently find the last position of a target element in a sorted array. It recursively narrows down the search space by adjusting the start and end indices based on whether the middle element is less than, equal to, or greater than the target.

```python
def last(arr, low, high, x):
    # Base case: If high index is less than low index
    if high < low:
        return -1

    # Find the middle index
    mid = (high + low) // 2

    # Check if the mid element is the target, return the mid index
    if arr[mid] == x:
        if mid == len(arr) - 1 or arr[mid + 1] != x:
            return mid
        # Recursive search in the right subarray
        return last(arr, mid + 1, high, x)
    # If the target is less than the mid element, search in the left subarray
    elif arr[mid] > x:
        return last(arr, low, mid - 1, x)
    # If the target is greater than the mid element, search in the right subarray
    else:
        return last(arr, mid + 1, high, x)
```
This function uses a binary search approach to efficiently find the last position of `x` in the sorted array `arr`. It starts by checking if the high index is less than the low index, in which case the target is not present in the array. It then calculates the middle index and recursively calls itself to search in the left or right subarray depending on whether the middle element is the target or not. If the target is found and it is not the last element in the subarray, it returns the current index.

