The `find_fixed_point` function implements the binary search algorithm to find a fixed point in the given list `nums`. It initializes two pointers, `left` and `right`, to the start and end of the list, respectively. In each iteration, it calculates the middle index `mid`. If the element at `mid` is equal to `mid`, it returns `mid` as the fixed point. If the element at `mid` is less than `mid`, it means the fixed point lies in the right half of the list, so the `left` pointer is moved to `mid + 1`. Otherwise, the fixed point lies in the left half, so the `right` pointer is moved to `mid - 1`. The loop continues until `left` exceeds `right`, at which point the function returns `-1` indicating that no fixed point exists in the list.

